message.content:
Your orchestratorPrompt describes the right conceptual workflow, including fetching PR data and invoking the three agents. The implementation in src/orchestrator.ts, however, does not use the Claude Agent SDK orchestration mechanism; it imports Anthropic from @anthropic-ai/sdk and calls this.client.messages.create(...), so the GitHub/ESLint MCP tools and subagents are never registered with the runtime.

✅ What you did well
src/prompts/orchestrator.prompt.ts explicitly says to fetch PR data with GitHub MCP tools and use the three named agents.
You import codeQualityAnalyzer, testCoverageAnalyzer, and refactoringSuggester in src/orchestrator.ts, which shows the intended design direction.
You validate the final response with ReviewReportSchema.parse(output), which is a strong safeguard after agent execution.
⚠️ What can be improved
Remove unused imports after the rewrite. Currently orchestratorPrompt and the three imported agent objects are not actually used in the client.messages.create() call.
Keep your JSON cleanup/validation approach, but apply it to the Agent SDK result event rather than a plain Messages API response.
❌ What is missing or incorrect
The current orchestrator does not meet the multi-agent execution requirement because src/orchestrator.ts uses this.client.messages.create({ ... }) from @anthropic-ai/sdk, and package.json does not include @anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk. There is no query() call, no mcpServers: mcpConfig.servers, no allowedTools containing GitHub/ESLint MCP tools plus Task, and no agents: [codeQualityAnalyzer, testCoverageAnalyzer, refactoringSuggester] registration. 👉 This is a core rubric requirement.

One concrete fix is:

import { query } from '@anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk';
import { mcpConfig } from './config/mcp.config.js';
import { codeQualityAnalyzer, testCoverageAnalyzer, refactoringSuggester } from './agents/index.js';

async reviewPullRequest(owner: string, repo: string, prNumber: number): Promise<ReviewReport> {
  const prompt = `${orchestratorPrompt}\nReview ${owner}/${repo}#${prNumber}. Fetch PR data, then use all three subagents.`;
  const stream = query({
    prompt,
    options: {
      mcpServers: mcpConfig.servers,
      allowedTools: ['mcp__github', 'mcp__eslint', 'Task', 'Read', 'Grep', 'Glob', 'Skill'],
      agents: [codeQualityAnalyzer, testCoverageAnalyzer, refactoringSuggester],
      maxTurns: 20,
    },
  });
  // collect the success result, parse JSON, then call ReviewReportSchema.parse(...)
}
Also update package.json with "@anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk": "^0.2.63" or newer. This registers tools and subagents with the orchestrator instead of asking a single model call to simulate them.

📚 How to Improve / Further Learning
Claude Agent SDK TypeScript repository(opens in a new tab) is the primary reference for query(), agents, allowed tools, and event handling.
Model Context Protocol servers repository(opens in a new tab) helps verify the exposed GitHub and ESLint tool names you should allow.
Anthropic tool use examples(opens in a new tab) provides conceptual reinforcement for tool registration and execution flow.
The main orchestrator agent in src/orchestrator.ts implements a class or function that utilizes the Claude Agent SDK's query (or equivalent unstable V2 client) method.
The orchestrator is configured with the allowedTools derived from the connected MCP servers (GitHub/ESLint).
The orchestrator is configured with the Task tool, this is an out of the box tool that spawns subagents.
All three subagents are registered in the agents array of the query configuration.
The orchestrator prompt (src/prompts/orchestrator.prompt.ts) explicitly instructs the model to fetch PR data and then invoke the subagents.

